#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kyora-image-build: build one version of a user image from its spec, in a builder VM.

  kyora-image-build --spec FILE [--base-digest sha256:...] [--digest sha256:...] [--name NAME]
                    [--base-ref REF] [--built-at ISO] [--base DEV|FILE|DIR] [--output DEV|FILE]
                    [--work DIR] [--timeout S] [--max-root-gb N] [--keep]

The builder VM boots kyora's `default` image with the base image attached read-only as
/dev/vdc and a blank sparse output drive as /dev/vdd (kernel command line
`kyora.build=base:<sectors>,output:<sectors>`, checked against both). The Worker runs this
as a root exec. It:

  1. mounts the base read-only (ro,noload) and an overlay on it, the upper on the scratch disk;
  2. runs rootfs/chroot-install.sh's delta phases in a chroot of the overlay: apt, the
     toolchains, cargo, npm, pip, uvTools, files, the run steps, /etc/kyora, the manifest,
     the strip. /tmp, /run, /work and /mnt are scratch, so nothing there reaches the image;
  3. checks that kyora-init, the agent and the /etc/kyora files it did not write are the base's;
  4. packs the flattened tree onto the output with `mkfs.ext4 -d`, deterministically: UUID
     and hash seed from the digest, the base's build time for every timestamp it wrote;
  5. checks it with `e2fsck -fn` and hashes it as vmd will (4 MiB chunks).

--base and --output also take an ext4 file or a directory, and a file, so it runs in an
ordinary sandbox too. stdout is NDJSON: log records {t, phase, step, stream, data} as the
build goes, then one result line, {"fsBytes": n, ...} or {"error": {"code", "message"}}.
Exit 0 (built), 1 (the build failed) or 2 (bad input: spec, digest, base or drives).
"""

